\chapter{Methodology}
\label{ch:method}

This chapter describes the design, implementation, and evaluation methodology used to develop the proposed intelligent traffic signal control system. The methodology follows a closed-loop control pattern: the simulator provides a state observation, the controller selects an action (signal decision), SUMO executes the action, and the system measures the resulting traffic performance.

\section{System Architecture and Simulation Environment}
The implementation is built around a SUMO--TraCI closed loop:
\[
s_t \xrightarrow{\pi} a_t \xrightarrow{\text{TraCI}} \text{SUMO step} \rightarrow s_{t+1},\; r_t
\]
where \(s_t\) is the observed traffic state at time \(t\), \(a_t\) is the selected control action, \(\pi\) is the control policy (baseline rule or learned policy), and \(r_t\) is the reward signal used during reinforcement learning.

The overall architecture (Figure~\ref{System diagram}) contains four functional blocks:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{SUMO Environment}: microscopic traffic dynamics, routing, and vehicle interactions.
    \item \textbf{Control Module}: baseline controllers and learning-based controllers that decide signal actions.
    \item \textbf{Replay Buffer}: stores experience transitions for off-policy learning.
    \item \textbf{DQN Networks}: online Q-network and a lagged target network for stable training.
\end{itemize}

