\section{SUMO: Microscopic Traffic Simulation}
The simulation environment uses SUMO (Simulation of Urban Mobility), an open-source microscopic traffic simulator. SUMO was selected because it:
\begin{itemize}
    \item provides vehicle-level motion and interaction models (car-following and lane-changing),
    \item supports emission and fuel-consumption estimation from motion profiles,
    \item can be controlled externally through TraCI, enabling closed-loop RL experiments.
\end{itemize}

\section{TraCI: Real-Time Control Interface}
TraCI enables real-time communication between Python and SUMO. In each step, the controller can:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Observe}: retrieve lane-level measurements (vehicle counts, halting/queue counts, mean speeds), and signal phase information.
    \item \textbf{Act}: set traffic light phases and phase durations for controlled intersections.
\end{itemize}

\section{Datasets and Map Construction (OSM Web Wizard)}
\label{sec:datasets}
\textbf{Map generation.} All city-scale maps used in this project were created using the \textbf{SUMO OSM Web Wizard}, which imports the road network from OpenStreetMap and generates a runnable SUMO scenario (network, routes, and configuration files).

\textbf{Traffic demand.} The traffic demand is \textbf{customized synthetic traffic} generated and tuned through the OSM Web Wizard parameters (e.g., cars/hour, trucks/hour, through-traffic factor). While some public transport route files (bus lines) are present in the generated scenarios, they are \textbf{Wizard-generated placeholders} and are not treated as real-world ground truth. They are used only as an additional simulated vehicle type and do not affect the interpretation of results as real-world public transport performance.

\textbf{Excluding the single-intersection dataset.} Although the repository contains a custom single-intersection scenario for debugging and rapid prototyping, the final evaluation in this report focuses on the OSM Web Wizard city networks; therefore, the single-intersection map is excluded from the experimental protocol and discussion.

\textbf{Controlled traffic lights.} City networks can contain many signalized intersections. Because of computing limits, only a configurable fraction of traffic lights is controlled during each run (ratio \(=0.5\) in the final experiments). All other traffic lights continue under SUMO’s default programs.

\section{Baseline Signal Control Strategies}
\label{sec:baselines}
This section defines the baseline controllers used to benchmark the learning-based controllers.

\subsection{Fixed-Time Controller (FTC)}
The Fixed-Time Controller is a non-adaptive benchmark with a predefined phase sequence and fixed green durations. Let \(p_t\) denote the current phase index in a sequence of length \(P\). The next phase is:
\[
p_{t+1} = (p_t + 1)\bmod P
\]
and each phase uses a fixed green time \(g_p\). This controller is stable and predictable but cannot adapt to fluctuating demand.

\textbf{Inputs/Outputs/Assumptions/Cost.}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Inputs}: current phase index \(p_t\); fixed timing plan \(\{g_p\}\).
    \item \textbf{Outputs}: next phase \(p_{t+1}\) and (fixed) phase duration \(g_{p_{t+1}}\).
    \item \textbf{Assumptions}: valid phase sequence exists for the controlled traffic light program.
    \item \textbf{Computational cost}: \(O(1)\) per decision.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Rule-Based Adaptive Controller (Queue Extension)}
The Adaptive baseline uses queue measurements and applies two decisions: \emph{extend} the current phase when demand persists, or \emph{switch} to the next phase in sequence when the current demand is low. Let \(\mathcal{L}(p)\) be the set of lanes served by phase \(p\). A weighted queue demand is:
\[
Q_p = \sum_{\ell\in \mathcal{L}(p)} \left(q_\ell + \alpha\, b_\ell\right)
\]
where \(q_\ell\) is the queue length (halting vehicles) on lane \(\ell\), \(b_\ell\) is the number of buses on \(\ell\), and \(\alpha>0\) assigns transit priority (buses contribute more to demand).

\textbf{Phase extension.} If the current phase has enough demand, the controller extends it by:
\[
\Delta g = \min\left(\Delta g_{\max},\; 2Q_{p_t} + \Delta g_{\text{bus}}\right)
\]
where \(\Delta g_{\text{bus}}\) adds extra green time when buses are detected (based on bus count and bus waiting).

\textbf{Switching.} Otherwise, the controller advances to the next phase in sequence and sets the green time as a bounded function of the next-phase queue (increase time for high queues, decrease time for low demand) while respecting minimum and maximum green constraints.

\textbf{Inputs/Outputs/Assumptions/Cost.}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Inputs}: lane queue lengths \(\{q_\ell\}\), bus counts \(\{b_\ell\}\), bus waiting times \(\{w^{(bus)}_\ell\}\), current phase \(p_t\), remaining time.
    \item \textbf{Outputs}: either extend the current phase by \(\Delta g\) or switch to the next phase and set a new duration.
    \item \textbf{Assumptions}: lane-to-phase mapping \(\mathcal{L}(p)\) is available (custom mapping or derived from controlled links on OSM maps).
    \item \textbf{Computational cost}: \(O(|\mathcal{L}(p)|)\) per decision.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{MaxPressure Controller}
MaxPressure is a strong queue-based baseline. For each candidate phase \(p\), a pressure score (queue proxy) is computed as:
\[
P_p = \sum_{\ell\in \mathcal{L}(p)} \left(q_\ell + \beta\,b_\ell + \eta\,w^{(bus)}_\ell\right)
\]
where \(w^{(bus)}_\ell\) is mean bus waiting time on lane \(\ell\), and \(\beta,\eta>0\) are bus-priority weights. The controller selects:
\[
p^\star = \arg\max_p P_p
\]
If \(p^\star=p_t\), it extends the current green; otherwise it switches to \(p^\star\) and sets a duration by normalizing \(P_{p^\star}\) into \([g_{\min}, g_{\max}]\). This baseline responds to congestion and often works well with mixed traffic flows.

\textbf{Inputs/Outputs/Assumptions/Cost.}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Inputs}: lane queue lengths, (optional) bus counts and bus waiting times, candidate phases \(p\in\mathcal{P}\).
    \item \textbf{Outputs}: selected phase \(p^\star\) and corresponding green duration.
    \item \textbf{Assumptions}: phase set \(\mathcal{P}\) and lane mapping \(\mathcal{L}(p)\) are defined.
    \item \textbf{Computational cost}: \(O\!\left(\sum_{p\in\mathcal{P}}|\mathcal{L}(p)|\right)\) per decision.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{SOTL (Self-Organizing Traffic Lights)}
SOTL is a threshold-based baseline. It continues the current green unless a competing phase demand exceeds a threshold or a maximum-green cap is reached (to prevent starvation). With demand \(D_p\) defined similarly to queue-weighted pressure, switching occurs if:
\[
\max_{p\neq p_t} D_p \ge \theta \quad \text{or} \quad \text{green\_time}(p_t) \ge g_{\max}
\]

\textbf{Inputs/Outputs/Assumptions/Cost.}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Inputs}: queue-based demands \(\{D_p\}\), thresholds \(\theta\), min/max green constraints.
    \item \textbf{Outputs}: keep/extend current phase or switch to the best competing phase.
    \item \textbf{Assumptions}: demand is a meaningful proxy for urgency; max-green prevents starvation.
    \item \textbf{Computational cost}: similar to MaxPressure (phase-wise demand computation).
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Genetic Algorithm (GA) Baseline}
\textbf{Note:} A Genetic Algorithm baseline was implemented during early prototyping but was excluded from the final experimental comparison due to its high computational cost and the difficulty of ensuring a fair comparison with reactive controllers on large OSM networks. The four baselines used in the final evaluation are Fixed-Time, Adaptive, MaxPressure, and SOTL.

\section{Reinforcement Learning Controllers}
\label{sec:rl}
To go beyond fixed-time and hand-tuned rules, this project implements Deep Q-Learning. The goal is to learn an approximation \(Q_\theta(s,a)\) of the optimal action-value function \(Q^\star(s,a)\), which satisfies:
\[
Q^\star(s,a)=\mathbb{E}\left[r_t+\gamma\max_{a'} Q^\star(s_{t+1},a')\right]
\]

\subsection{Parameter-Sharing Multi-Agent DQN on OSM Networks (MARL\_DQN)}
In a city network, multiple traffic lights must be controlled. The approach used is \textbf{parameter sharing}: all controlled traffic lights share one DQN (shared weights and shared replay buffer). Each traffic light \(i\) has its own local state \(s_t^{(i)}\) and chooses an action \(a_t^{(i)}\), but all experiences update the same network.

\textbf{Inputs/Outputs/Assumptions/Cost.}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Inputs}: per-traffic-light local state vector \(s_t^{(i)}\in\mathbb{R}^{77}\) (lane counts/queues/speeds for \(K=20\) padded lanes + 16-bin phase one-hot + remaining time), decision interval \(\Delta t\), controlled-TL set size \(N_{\mathrm{TL}}\).
    \item \textbf{Outputs}: per-traffic-light discrete action \(a_t^{(i)}\in\{0,1\}\) (keep vs next phase), applied via TraCI.
    \item \textbf{Assumptions}: local observation is enough (partial observability); fixed lane padding \(K=20\) is sufficient for each intersection; the 2-action design applies across different OSM phase programs.
    \item \textbf{Computational cost}: inference cost scales as \(O(N_{\mathrm{TL}}\cdot f_{\theta})\) per decision, where \(f_{\theta}\) is one forward-pass through the shared network. Training adds minibatch updates (replay sampling + backprop) per decision step.
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{Local State Representation}
For each controlled traffic light \(i\), the simulator retrieves the set of controlled incoming lanes and enforces a fixed input size by padding/truncating to \(K=20\) lanes. For each lane \(\ell\), three features are used: vehicle count \(n_\ell\), queue length \(q_\ell\), and mean speed \(v_\ell\). These are normalized and concatenated with a one-hot encoding of the current phase \(\phi_t\) (16 bins) and the normalized remaining time \(\tau_t\):
\[
s_t^{(i)}=
\Big[
\tilde{n}_{1:K},\;
\tilde{q}_{1:K},\;
\tilde{v}_{1:K},\;
\text{onehot}(\phi_t),\;
\tilde{\tau}_t
\Big]
\]
Hence, the OSM observation dimension is:
\[
d = 3K + 16 + 1 = 77
\]

\subsubsection{Action Space}
To work with different phase programs across intersections, the MARL controller uses a simple 2-action space:
\[
a_t^{(i)}\in\{0,1\}
\]
where \(a=0\) keeps the current phase and \(a=1\) switches to the next SUMO phase (cyclic). This choice improves stability across different intersections, but limits flexibility because the policy cannot jump directly to an arbitrary phase.

\subsubsection{Reward Function (Waiting Time + Queue Reduction + Switching Penalty)}
The local reward is designed to minimize vehicle waiting time while encouraging queue clearance and discouraging unnecessary switching:
\[
r_t^{(i)} = -0.50\,\hat{W}_t^{(i)} + 0.35\,\Delta Q_t^{(i)} - 0.05\cdot \mathbb{I}[a_t^{(i)}=1]
\]
where \(\hat{W}_t^{(i)} = \text{clip}(\bar{w}_t^{(i)}/200,\;0,\;1)\) is the normalized mean lane waiting time, \(\Delta Q_t^{(i)} = \text{clip}((Q_{t-1}^{(i)} - Q_t^{(i)})/10,\;-1,\;1)\) is the normalized queue reduction, and the indicator penalizes phase switches. The dominant waiting-time term directly optimizes for the primary evaluation metric.

\subsection{Dueling Double DQN with \(n\)-step Returns}
To improve stability and training efficiency, the implemented DQN includes:

\textbf{Dueling architecture.} The Q-value is decomposed into value and advantage streams:
\[
Q_\theta(s,a)=V_\theta(s)+\left(A_\theta(s,a)-\frac{1}{|\mathcal{A}|}\sum_{a'}A_\theta(s,a')\right)
\]

\textbf{Double DQN target.} To reduce overestimation bias, the next action is selected by the online network but evaluated by the target network:
\[
y_t=r_t+\gamma\,Q_{\theta^-}\!\left(s_{t+1},\arg\max_{a'}Q_\theta(s_{t+1},a')\right)
\]
The objective minimized is the mean-squared TD error:
\[
\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}\left[(y_t-Q_\theta(s_t,a_t))^2\right]
\]

\textbf{\(n\)-step returns.} Experience can be stored as an \(n\)-step return to speed up credit assignment:
\[
R_t^{(n)}=\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\gamma^k r_{t+k}
\]
and the learning update uses \(R_t^{(n)}\) with the \(n\)-step successor state.

\textbf{Replay buffer and target update.} Transitions are sampled uniformly from a replay buffer. The target network \(\theta^-\) is synchronized with the online network \(\theta\) every \(C\) updates.

\textbf{Stabilization techniques.} To prevent Q-value overestimation and ensure stable training, the following techniques are applied: (i) hard target network updates every \(C=2000\) steps (replacing soft Polyak averaging); (ii) Q-target clamping to \([-2,\;2]\); (iii) gradient clipping with max norm \(0.5\); and (iv) Layer Normalization after each hidden layer of both value and advantage streams.

\textbf{Exploration.} Training uses an \(\epsilon\)-greedy policy:
\[
a_t=
\begin{cases}
\text{Uniform}(\mathcal{A}) & \text{with probability }\epsilon\\
\arg\max_a Q_\theta(s_t,a) & \text{with probability }1-\epsilon
\end{cases}
\]
with \(\epsilon\) annealed from \(\epsilon_{\text{start}}=1.0\) to \(\epsilon_{\min}=0.05\) over the course of training.

\textbf{Network architecture.} The Q-network uses a three-layer fully-connected MLP with hidden dimensions [256, 256, 128]. All layers use ReLU activation functions, and weights are initialized using Xavier uniform initialization. Layer Normalization is applied after each hidden layer to stabilize training. The dueling architecture splits the final hidden layer into separate value and advantage streams before combining them.

\textbf{Key hyperparameters (to report).} Learning rate \(\alpha\), discount \(\gamma\), replay size \(N\), batch size \(B\), target update period \(C\), \(n\)-step horizon \(n\), and network architecture should be explicitly stated (Table~\ref{tab:exp_setup}) to ensure fair comparison and reproducibility.

\begin{algorithm}
\caption{Shared Multi-Agent Dueling Double DQN Training (OSM networks)}
\label{alg:marl_dqn}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
    \State Initialize shared replay buffer \(D\), online network \(Q_\theta\), target network \(Q_{\theta^-}\leftarrow Q_\theta\)
    \For{episode \(=1\) to \(M\)}
        \State Reset SUMO; select controlled traffic lights \(\{1,\dots,N_{\mathrm{TL}}\}\)
        \For{decision step \(t\)}
            \For{each traffic light \(i\)}
                \State Observe local state \(s_t^{(i)}\); select \(a_t^{(i)}\) by \(\epsilon\)-greedy on \(Q_\theta\)
                \State Apply action via TraCI (keep phase or switch to next phase)
            \EndFor
            \For{each traffic light \(i\)}
                \State Observe \(s_{t+1}^{(i)}\), compute \(r_t^{(i)}\), store \((s_t^{(i)},a_t^{(i)},r_t^{(i)},s_{t+1}^{(i)})\) in \(D\)
            \EndFor
            \State Sample mini-batch from \(D\); compute Double DQN targets \(y_t\); update \(\theta\) by minimizing \(\mathcal{L}(\theta)\)
            \If{update step \(\bmod C=0\)} \State \(\theta^- \leftarrow \theta\) \EndIf
        \EndFor
    \EndFor
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\section{Experimental Protocol and Performance Metrics}
\label{sec:metrics}
Experiments are run by executing each controller on an OSM city map for a fixed simulation horizon \(T_{\text{sim}}\). To support fair comparison and reproducibility, all strategies share the same simulation horizon, decision interval, controlled-traffic-light selection rule, and measurement pipeline. Following common practice, stochasticity is controlled using explicit SUMO random seeds, so that independent episodes can be repeated and statistics reported.

